==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
First attested [[w:1382|1382]], from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|cocatriz|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{term/t|la|calcātrīx||she who treads upon something}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term/t|la|calcō||tread}}, from {{term/t|la|calx||heel, hoof}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|heraldry|mythology|lang=en}} A legendary creature about the size and shape of a [[dragon]] or [[wyvern]], but in appearance resembling a giant [[rooster]], with some lizard-like characteristics.
#* {{rfdate}} [[w:J. Walker McSpadden|J. Walker McSpadden]], ''The Spell of Egypt''
#*: ''“Peace reigns in happy Luxor. The lion lies down with the lamb, and the child, if it will, may harmlessly put its hand into the '''cockatrice’s''' den”''
# Mistress, harlot

===References===
* For meaning "mistress": '''1949''', John Dover Wilson (compiler), '''Life in Shakespeare's England. A Book of Elizabethan Prose''', Cambridge at the University Press. 1st ed. 1911, 2nd ed. 1913, 8th reprint. In Glossary and Notes

